; ==========================================================================
; STELLAR PONG  -  you on the RIGHT, the computer on the left   (VM1 @ $4000)
; ==========================================================================
; Your paddle is the one on the RIGHT.  UP and DOWN arrows move it (w and s work
; too, for terminals that send no arrow keys).  First to 9 wins.  Q quits.
;
; NO SIGNED ARITHMETIC ANYWHERE.  Positions are magnitudes and each axis carries
; a direction FLAG; a bounce flips the flag.  That is Bouncing-Ball's model, and
; it sidesteps SUBXY leaving a magnitude with its sign in a separate byte.
;
; THE COURT.  Line 1 is the score, lines 2 and 23 are the walls, and the ball
; lives on lines 3..22.  Your paddle is column 78, the computer's column 3, four
; cells each.  The ball turns at columns 77 and 4, so it visibly reaches a paddle
; before coming back.
;
; SPIN - where the ball strikes the paddle decides which way it leaves: off the
; top cell it goes up, off the bottom cell down, off the middle two it carries
; on.  This is your only control over the ball, and it is also what ends rallies.
; Without it the ball keeps a 45 degree path for ever and both paddles can track
; it indefinitely: measured, 628 frames and still nil-nil.
;
; THE COMPUTER only starts tracking when the ball is within 8 columns of its own
; end; the rest of the time it holds position.  It also steps only on the frames
; the ball steps, so that number IS how many rows of correction it can make
; before the ball arrives - a real difficulty knob, monotonic in both directions.
; An earlier version gated the paddle on a frame counter (move one cell every Nth
; frame) and that measured NON-MONOTONIC: N=3 and N=5 lost, N=4 won 9-2 - a frame
; counter phase-locks against the ball's own path rather than capping skill.  The
; operand is the COLUMN it starts tracking at, 12, at $4288.  The computer guards
; the LEFT end, so RAISE it to widen the band and make the computer harder, lower
; it to make it easier.
;
; FLICKER.  A paddle only ever moves one cell, so exactly ONE cell is stale -
; moving up the old bottom, moving down the old top.  Erase that one and draw the
; new leading cell; never redraw the paddle.  The ball is erased before it moves
; and drawn after.  About 30 bytes of output per frame.
;
; $89 TESTLXY IS STRICTLY LESS THAN, NOT "LESS THAN OR EQUAL".  The COMMANDS
; docx says "Less Than or Equal to"; measured on V0061.24 it is the exact
; complement of $88, which really is >=:
;
;                X<Y   X=Y   X>Y
;      $88        F     T     T     >=  (as documented)
;      $89        T     F     F     <   (documented as <=)
;
; That cost the paddle its BOTTOM CELL - drawn, lit, and never returning the
; ball, because the range test excluded ball == top+3.  It also made SPINDN
; unreachable, so the ball could be sent up off the top cell but never down off
; the bottom one.  Every "<=" here is now $88 with the operands the other way
; round: TESTGXY R10 R2 is R10 >= R2, which is R2 <= R10.  Both paddles use the
; same test, so both were a cell short.
;
; $11 LOADXI CANNOT WRITE R0.  `11 00 nn nn` silently does nothing - measured on
; V0061.24, and the reason the first build of this program hung: a wall-drawing
; loop counted down from a value that was never loaded.  Every other route in
; works (COPYAX, COPYXY, INCX, ADDXI), so where this program wants a constant in
; R0 it goes LOADAI / COPYAX instead.  Bouncing-Ball never noticed because the
; only value it ever loaded into R0 was zero, which is what R0 already held.
;
; CALL DEPTH 2.  CELL does its own cursor positioning rather than calling POSXY,
; so the deepest chain is main -> FULLP -> CELL.  Depth 2 is proven
; (Tic-Tac-Toe); depth 3 is not, and this does not need it.
;
; THE BALL STEPS EVERY OTHER FRAME (on).  Keys are still read every frame, so
; your paddle gets TWO cells per ball cell - that is what stops it feeling
; sluggish, and unlike a bigger delay it does not slow the key response with it.
; The computer is deliberately held to the ball's frames, so REACT keeps meaning
; ball-steps.  The tick byte is at $4901.
;
; THE FRAME DELAY at $4318 (DLYI 14) is the ball's speed knob, and it has to be
; big enough to DOMINATE the frame: a held frame does less work than a stepping
; one, so with the old DLYI 03 holding the ball bought only 1.23x.  At 14 it
; buys 1.46x, and with the hold the ball is 2.1x slower than the first build.
;
; ONE CELL A FRAME.  The key loop drains everything waiting, so without a cap a
; terminal repeating a held arrow key teleports your paddle across the court.
; R9 counts the cells moved this frame and PCAP (1) is the allowance: spend it
; and the rest of the frame runs, leaving the unread keys in the UART for the
; next one - a burst is SLOWED, not thrown away.  The value is measured, not
; guessed: against the same auto-player, 2 cells a frame gave 0-2 in 1159 frames
; and 3 or more gave 0-0 stalemates - both paddles track a 45 degree ball
; perfectly and no rally ever ends, the same failure SPIN exists to prevent.
; At 1 the game actually reaches a result.  The operand is at $4160.
;
; REGISTERS.  R0 scratch, R1 ball column, R2 ball line, R3 ball x-direction
; (0 left, 1 right), R4 ball y-direction (0 up, 1 down), R5 your paddle top line
; (right side), R6 the computer's (left side), R7 your score, R8 its score, R9 your paddle moves left this
; frame, RA scratch, RB memory pointer, RC the key, RD/RE line and column for
; POSXY, RF the constant ten.
;
; VERIFICATION (Firmware-Math/pong_verify.py, real V0061.24 sim): the escape
; stream is replayed into an 80x24 screen after every cell change, and at the
; start of every frame the ball is inside the court, both paddles are four
; contiguous cells on their own columns, and nothing else is lit.  Clean over
; 1800+ ball-steps, and with no one at the keyboard the computer wins 9-0 and the
; program exits.  `--play` then drives the arrow keys from the ball's position and
; plays the game, which is what proves the input path.  With the ball held to
; every other frame that auto-player - which tracks the ball almost perfectly -
; is held to 1-1 over 1868 ball-steps where the first build lost 5-9, so rallies
; are much longer now.  A human is not that auto-player; if games drag on the
; hardware, narrow the computer's band with the REACT operand above.
;
; HARDWARE-VERIFIED on the Elf2K, 2026-08-25 - flashed and played, and it plays
; nicely.  That run covers the swapped sides, the half-speed ball, the one-cell
; paddle cap and the $89 TESTLXY fix that gave the paddles back their bottom
; cell.  The tuning shipped here (REACT, the frame delay, PCAP) is the tuning
; that was played.
;
; 414 STELLAR commands, $4000-$4445 (1094 bytes of code).
; Data at $4900, 161 bytes, past the end marker.
;
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DISASSEMBLY  -  commented out; the loader skips it
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------

; D 4000 CD             START                  Start of program, and run the label pre-pass
; D 4001 11 0F 0A 00    LOADXI R15 0A00        The instructions
; D 4005 C1 0F          PRINTSTR R15
; D 4007 C3             RAND                   Spin the generator while we wait - the number of turns it
; D 4008 CC             SERST                  gets depends on when the operator presses a key, and that
; D 4009 A1 00 07       IFFALSEI SEED          is the only thing that differs between two runs
; D 400C CB             SERIN                  Take the key and throw it away
